Title: Define and test attachment format coverage
Created: 2026-09-02
Author: codex

--- log ---
2026-09-02T16:03Z codex created

2026-09-02T16:04Z codex note author requested the attachment-format policy and fixture matrix be ticketed
--- body ---
## Context

The specification has an explicit PDF path and a format-neutral consumer of
Zotero's `/fulltext`, but no attachment-format policy. Zotero's current search
documentation says it indexes PDF, EPUB, HTML and plain-text files and does not
index other document types such as DOCX and ODT. The chain does not say what
happens to DOC, DOCX, ODT, TXT, Markdown, RTF or HTML attachments, whether
eligibility follows extension or MIME type, or what R17 reports when body text
is unsupported.

This ticket establishes evidence first and carries the resulting policy to a
ruling. It does not authorize adding LibreOffice, Pandoc, Tika, a cloud
converter, or any other document-conversion runtime.

Primary platform reference:
`https://www.zotero.org/support/preferences/search`.

## Candidate policy to test and rule

- Eligibility follows detected MIME/content capability, never filename
  extension alone.
- PDF uses Zotero text plus the ruled pdf.js structure/page path.
- EPUB and HTML initially use Zotero-extracted flat text; source-aware structure
  is separate measured work.
- Plain text enters directly. TXT and Markdown are probed separately because a
  `.md` attachment need not be exposed as `text/plain`.
- DOC, DOCX, ODT and RTF remain metadata-searchable when Zotero supplies no body
  text, with the stored reason `unsupported-format` rather than an unexplained
  absence or a false indexed state.
- A future local converter needs a separate ruling over security, resource
  budgets, fidelity and derivation identity. Remote conversion is never an
  implicit fallback under R10.

## Actions

1. Build a minimal fixture matrix: PDF, EPUB, HTML, TXT, Markdown, DOC, DOCX,
   ODT and RTF, each with a unique body token and ordinary record metadata.
2. Import each through normal Zotero, record the attachment content type, ask
   Zotero to reindex it, and probe `/fulltext` plus the full-text control
   plugin's state.
3. Record for each fixture: extension, reported MIME type, whether Zotero
   exposes body text, whether the unique token survives, and the observed
   terminal state.
4. Put the candidate policy to the author with every observed exception. Land
   the ruling in `DECISIONS.md` before changing SPEC.md.
5. Add an extraction-route/reason field per attachment and R17 counts for at
   least `zotero-fulltext`, `pdf-source`, `unsupported-format`, `unavailable`
   and `failed`, using the final ruled vocabulary.
6. Add acceptance fixtures proving metadata remains searchable for every
   format and body search behaves exactly as the matrix declares.

## Test

- One unique body token per format distinguishes real extraction from a pass
  based only on shared metadata.
- Rename at least one fixture to a misleading extension while preserving its
  detected content type; extension-only dispatch must fail the control.
- An unsupported body is metadata-searchable and reports its reason.
- A supported body is searchable and records the extraction route that
  produced it.
- No test invokes a network or document-conversion service.

## Verification

- [ ] The raw Zotero responses and fixture hashes are committed as evidence.
- [ ] Markdown and RTF are classified by observation, not assumption.
- [ ] Every named format has a positive metadata-search control.
- [ ] Every supported format has a body-token control.
- [ ] Every unsupported format has an explicit-reason control.
- [ ] `make check` and `./tickets/erg check` pass.

## Invariants

- Metadata coverage never depends on body-format support.
- Unsupported is a terminal, reported state; it is not failure and not indexed.
- No converter joins the default path by implication.
- The policy is MIME/capability based, not an extension allow-list.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The nine-format fixture matrix is measured and committed.
- [ ] The author ratifies the attachment-format policy in `DECISIONS.md`.
- [ ] SPEC.md states eligibility, fallbacks and reporting once, in their owning
      sections.
- [ ] R17 reports extraction route and explicit unsupported reasons.
- [ ] Acceptance coverage matches the ruled matrix.
